From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] AP MLD: one AID bit per sta_info, and a peer interface that belongs to the station

Two Multi-AP backhaul agents associating to one AP MLD were handed the same AID.
The 4-address peer interface name is derived from it -- hostapd_set_wds_sta()
builds "%s.sta%d" from conf->iface and aid - 64 -- so the second station was
pointed at an interface the first one owned:

  hostapd: ap-mld-2: STA 3e:fb:2f:94:72:7b associated (aid 65)
  hostapd: ap-mld-2: WDS-STA-INTERFACE-ADDED ifname=ap-mld-2.sta1
  hostapd: ap-mld-2: STA 06:0c:43:26:81:10 associated (aid 65)
  hostapd: nl80211: NL80211_ATTR_STA_VLAN (addr=06:0c:43:26:81:10
           ifname=ap-mld-2.sta1 vlan_id=0) failed: -16 (Resource busy)
  hostapd: ap-mld-2: AP-STA-CONNECTED 06:0c:43:26:81:10 auth_alg=sae

hostapd reports the station connected regardless, so it stays associated on both
its links, fully authorized, reporting good signal -- and moves not one byte,
because it has no bridged interface. Measured on hardware 2026-07-30; every
status anyone could query said the node was healthy.

There are two independent reasons the AID came out duplicated, and this fixes
both by making one thing true: the bit for an AID lives in the transmitted BSS
of whichever BSS owns that sta_info, every sta_info sets exactly one bit and
clears exactly that one, and the allocator ORs across the affiliated links.

  - hostapd_get_aid() allocates into hostapd_mbssid_get_tx_bss(hapd)->sta_aid
    and ap_free_sta() clears it there, but hostapd_get_aid_word() checked
    link_bss->sta_aid directly. With mbssid=1 the backhaul AP MLD is a
    non-transmitted BSS, so an allocation made through the transmitted BSS was
    invisible to the check and two stations with different association links
    could be given the same AID immediately, with nothing having been freed.
    Read the bits where they are written.

  - hostapd_get_aid() redirects hapd to the transmitted BSS before
    hostapd_get_aid_word() ever sees it, so hapd->conf->mld_ap was that of the
    transmitted BSS rather than of the AP MLD, and the cross-link OR was skipped
    for a non-transmitted MLD. Keep the BSS the caller passed in for the MLD
    question and use the transmitted one only for storage.

  - ieee80211_ml_process_link() marked hapd->sta_aid on the link BSS while
    ap_free_sta() clears tx_bss->sta_aid. Marked in one place, cleared in
    another: the AID could be handed out again while a link still held it, and
    the release could clear a bit belonging to a station on the transmitted BSS.
    Mark it where it will be cleared.

The peer interface is a separate matter and is fixed separately. netifd creates
a single netdev for the whole MLD, so every link BSS shares conf->iface and
derives the same interface name; tearing one link down therefore removes the
interface the station is still using on its remaining links and rebinds its VLAN
back to the BSS. Guard that teardown with hostapd_sta_is_link_sta(), the same
distinction __ap_free_sta() makes three lines further down.

Deliberately not guarded: the AID release itself. An earlier version of this
patch did guard it, which leaks -- the bit set by ieee80211_ml_process_link() is
cleared in exactly one place in the tree, so refusing to clear it there means it
is never cleared at all, and every MLD association permanently consumes one AID
out of 65..2007 until hostapd starts refusing stations. With ownership symmetric
no predicate is needed: each sta_info clears its own bit and the AID becomes free
when the last one goes.

Companion to the AID offset fix in ieee80211_ml_process_link(): that one made the
marking land in the right word, this one makes it land in the right BSS.

Signed-off-by: WOZIWRT <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
diff -u -r a/src/ap/ieee802_11.c b/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
--- a/src/ap/ieee802_11.c	2026-07-30 10:39:27.371537767 +0200
+++ b/src/ap/ieee802_11.c	2026-07-30 10:39:27.372489080 +0200
@@ -3963,7 +3963,13 @@
 			if (!link_bss)
 				continue;
 
-			aid_word |= link_bss->sta_aid[i];
+			/* Read the bits where they are written. Allocation
+			 * and release both go through the transmitted BSS, so
+			 * looking at link_bss->sta_aid misses every AID taken
+			 * by a non-transmitted BSS -- with mbssid=1 that is
+			 * exactly the backhaul AP MLD, and two stations then
+			 * get the same AID with nothing having been freed. */
+			aid_word |= hostapd_mbssid_get_tx_bss(link_bss)->sta_aid[i];
 		}
 
 		return aid_word;
@@ -3976,11 +3982,14 @@
 
 int hostapd_get_aid(struct hostapd_data *hapd, struct sta_info *sta)
 {
+	struct hostapd_data *mld_hapd = hapd;
 	int i, j = 32, aid;
 
 	/* Transmitted and non-transmitted BSSIDs share the same AID pool, so
 	 * use the shared storage in the transmitted BSS to find the next
-	 * available value. */
+	 * available value. Keep the BSS we were called on as well: it is the
+	 * one that knows whether this is an AP MLD, and the transmitted BSS
+	 * need not be part of the same MLD. */
 	hapd = hostapd_mbssid_get_tx_bss(hapd);
 
 	/* get a unique AID */
@@ -3993,7 +4002,7 @@
 		return -1;
 
 	for (i = 0; i < AID_WORDS; i++) {
-		u32 aid_word = hostapd_get_aid_word(hapd, sta, i);
+		u32 aid_word = hostapd_get_aid_word(mld_hapd, sta, i);
 
 		if (aid_word == (u32) -1)
 			continue;
@@ -5399,7 +5408,10 @@
 			status = WLAN_STATUS_UNSPECIFIED_FAILURE;
 			goto out;
 		}
-		hapd->sta_aid[(sta->aid - 1 - 64) / 32] |=
+		/* Mark it where ap_free_sta() will clear it. Every sta_info
+		 * of this MLD sets exactly one bit and clears that same one,
+		 * so the AID stays taken until the last of them is gone. */
+		hostapd_mbssid_get_tx_bss(hapd)->sta_aid[(sta->aid - 1 - 64) / 32] |=
 			BIT((sta->aid - 1 - 64) % 32);
 		sta->listen_interval = origin_sta->listen_interval;
 		if (update_ht_state(hapd, sta) > 0 && set_beacon)
diff -u -r a/src/ap/sta_info.c b/src/ap/sta_info.c
--- a/src/ap/sta_info.c	2026-07-30 10:39:27.371148637 +0200
+++ b/src/ap/sta_info.c	2026-07-30 10:39:27.372085719 +0200
@@ -306,11 +306,20 @@
 	ap_sta_set_authorized(hapd, sta, 0);
 	hostapd_set_sta_flags(hapd, sta);
 
-	if ((sta->flags & WLAN_STA_WDS) ||
-	    (sta->flags & WLAN_STA_MULTI_AP &&
-	     (hapd->conf->multi_ap & BACKHAUL_BSS) &&
-	     hapd->conf->wds_sta &&
-	     !(sta->flags & WLAN_STA_WPS)))
+	/* A non-AP MLD has one sta_info per affiliated link on top of the one
+	 * the association was performed on. The 4-address peer interface,
+	 * however, belongs to the station: netifd creates a single netdev for
+	 * the whole MLD, so every link BSS shares conf->iface and
+	 * hostapd_set_wds_sta() derives the very same name on each of them.
+	 * Tearing one link down therefore takes away the interface the station
+	 * is still using on its remaining links, and rebinds its VLAN back to
+	 * the BSS. __ap_free_sta() below already makes this distinction. */
+	if (!hostapd_sta_is_link_sta(hapd, sta) &&
+	    ((sta->flags & WLAN_STA_WDS) ||
+	     (sta->flags & WLAN_STA_MULTI_AP &&
+	      (hapd->conf->multi_ap & BACKHAUL_BSS) &&
+	      hapd->conf->wds_sta &&
+	      !(sta->flags & WLAN_STA_WPS))))
 		hostapd_set_wds_sta(hapd, NULL, sta->addr, sta->aid, 0);
 
 	if (sta->ipaddr)
